    now, along with upstream's `throw std::runtime_error("not implemented")`
    under `#if USE_INPUT_KV`. The replacement is `driver-cuda/src/fire/xqa.rs`
    and always was.
  * **`mha.cuh:1455` reads `result.fill(AccType{})` where upstream writes
    `AccType{0, 0}`**, in `smemFp16ArraySum`. This is the only change in
    either tree that REPLACES upstream's bytes in place, and the only one that
    carries NO `// PIE:` marker. Everywhere else our text is added beside
    upstream's rather than over it -- `attention/mla.cuh`'s two `.template`
    sites keep upstream's spelling inside the `#ifndef` and put ours in an
    `#else`, which is why step 1 can still recover the file -- or it removes
    text a marker names. Here upstream's spelling is simply not there. It was
    made at `2af552a24`, while getting XQA's device half to compile at all,
    and no ledger was re-run for it.

    It is OUR shim that made it necessary, not upstream. `AccType` is
    `mha::conditional_t<useFp32Acc, float2, InputElem2>`, and for the bf16
    lattice `INPUT_ELEM2` is `__nv_bfloat162` (`xqa/defines.h:41`), which
    `csrc/shim/cuda_bf16.h:262` aliases to the prelude's `device::bf16x2` --
    `struct { bf16 x; bf16 y; }`, whose element type declares its
    constructors `explicit` on purpose so that a stray `bf16 b = 0;` is
    refused. A braced `{0, 0}` copy-initialises each member and therefore
    cannot see an `explicit` constructor, while NVIDIA's own `__nv_bfloat162`
    takes it. `AccType{}` value-initialises and is zero for both arms. That is
    the reason `2af552a24` records and the type definitions corroborate; it
    was not compiled against NVIDIA's headers to check the converse.

    It costs 4 bytes and no lines, which is exactly why the byte column caught
    it and the line column did not. A rebase must re-apply it;
    `MODIFICATIONS` carries it as a numbered modification for that reason.
