CCCL, NVIDIA CUDA headers
-------------------------

Neither is carried, and that is the point of `csrc/shim`. Where
`examples/halftype_parity.rs` reads NVIDIA's `cuda_fp16.h` and `cuda_bf16.h`
from `$CUDA_HOME`, it does so at RUN time, in a probe, to separate "is this
source acceptable to NVRTC" from "is our shim correct". No toolkit text is
carried in this crate's binary and none is redistributed.

The probe that also read FlashInfer's own CCCL checkout was
`examples/flashinfer_probe.rs`, deleted at `1a08b179a` with sixteen other
examples. Only the NVIDIA half of this paragraph still has a running witness;
the CCCL half is now an unmeasured claim, which does not weaken it -- nothing
carried has a `cccl` or `cutlass` path, and `csrc/shim` holds no file under
either -- but it is worth saying which half is checked.


The two LICENSE files
---------------------

`csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/LICENSE` and `csrc/src/attn/xqa/LICENSE` are
byte-identical to each other: both are the stock Apache License 2.0 text, 230
lines, as each upstream ships it. They are kept as two files rather than one
shared copy because each is the licence *of its own directory* and a rebase
replaces a directory, not a crate. Neither may be renamed: `carried.rs`
excludes a file from the NVRTC header set by basename -- `LICENSE`, `NOTICE`,
`MODIFICATIONS`, `README`, `README.md` -- so a name like `FLASHINFER-LICENSE`
would quietly put 12 KB of licence text into `includeNames[]`.
